      "output": "<FINAL_JSON>\n{\n  \"schema_version\": \"legal-title-vii-answer/v1\",\n  \"legal_as_of\": \"2025-03-01\",\n  \"jurisdiction_scope\": \"US_federal_Title_VII_only\",\n  \"source_resolution\": {\n    \"discrimination_controlling_source\": \"SRC-SCOTUS-MULDROW-2024\",\n    \"stale_source_treatment\": \"vacated_not_controlling_for_discrimination_harm\",\n    \"retaliation_controlling_source\": \"SRC-SCOTUS-BURLINGTON-2006\",\n    \"discrimination_harm_standard\": \"some_harm_to_terms_or_conditions_no_significance_test\",\n    \"retaliation_harm_standard\": \"material_adversity_reasonable_worker_deterrence\"\n  },\n  \"issue_outcomes\": {\n    \"coverage\": {\n      \"conclusion\": \"covered\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"SRC-STATUTE-2000E\", \"SRC-SCOTUS-WALTERS-1997\"],\n      \"fact_ids\": [\"F-PAYROLL-16\", \"F-PARTTIME-FRIDAY\", \"F-WEEKS-22\"],\n      \"analysis\": \"Northwind maintained 16 employment relationships across 22 weeks in both years. Under Walters, the payroll method counts relationships, not daily work or pay. The two part-timers count on Fridays. Northwind meets the 15/20 threshold.\"\n    },\n    \"discrimination_rivera\": {\n      \"conclusion\": \"plausible_claim_not_ultimate_liability\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"SRC-STATUTE-2000E-2\", \"SRC-SCOTUS-MULDROW-2024\"],\n      \"fact_ids\": [\"F-RIVERA-TRANSFER\", \"F-MALE-REPLACEMENT\", \"F-MOTIVE-SEX\"],\n      \"analysis\": \"Post-Muldrow, transfer discrimination requires some injury to terms/conditions, not significant harm. Rivera lost schedule, supervisory work, and vehicle; a less experienced male replaced her; a preserved message prefers a man for the role. Equal pay does not defeat the claim. Authentication of the message is unassessed.\"\n    },\n    \"retaliation_rivera\": {\n      \"conclusion\": \"plausible_claim_material_adversity_required\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"SRC-STATUTE-2000E-3\", \"SRC-SCOTUS-BURLINGTON-2006\"],\n      \"fact_ids\": [\"F-RIVERA-PROTECTED-COMPLAINT\", \"F-RIVERA-TRANSFER\", \"F-MOTIVE-COMPLAINT\"],\n      \"analysis\": \"Rivera's sex-discrimination complaint is protected opposition. The transfer followed within 9 days; an HR note attributes it to reducing friction. Under Burlington, retaliation requires material adversity that might dissuade a reasonable worker. Loss of schedule, supervisory work, and vehicle plausibly meets that standard. Muldrow's some-injury test is discrimination-specific.\"\n    },\n    \"timeliness_rivera\": {\n      \"conclusion\": \"timely_under_300_day_fepa_window\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"SRC-STATUTE-2000E-5\", \"SRC-EEOC-FILING-CHARGE\"],\n      \"fact_ids\": [\"F-FILING-295\", \"F-NOTICE-DATE\", \"F-FEPA\", \"F-NO-FEPA-TERMINATION\"],\n      \"analysis\": \"The 2025-02-20 filing occurred 295 days after the 2024-05-01 final notice. Where an authorized state agency exists, the deadline extends to 300 days. No termination notice was received. The filing is timely.\"\n    },\n    \"retaliation_lee\": {\n      \"conclusion\": \"no_title_vii_protected_activity_on_supplied_facts\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"SRC-STATUTE-2000E-3\"],\n      \"fact_ids\": [\"F-LEE-SAFETY-ONLY\"],\n      \"analysis\": \"Title VII retaliation requires opposition to a practice unlawful under Title VII or participation in a Title VII proceeding. Lee complained only about physical safety and never opposed sex or other enumerated discrimination. Safety complaints are not Title VII-protected activity on these facts.\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"trap_resolutions\": {\n    \"T-STALE-CA8\": {\n      \"verdict\": \"rejected\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"SRC-SCOTUS-MULDROW-2024\"],\n      \"fact_ids\": [\"F-RIVERA-TRANSFER\"],\n      \"reason\": \"The Eighth Circuit's materially-significant-disadvantage standard was vacated by the Supreme Court in Muldrow (2024); equal pay does not defeat a transfer claim where some injury to terms/conditions exists.\"\n    },\n    \"T-STANDARDS-CONFLATED\": {\n      \"verdict\": \"rejected\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"SRC-SCOTUS-MULDROW-2024\", \"SRC-SCOTUS-BURLINGTON-2006\"],\n      \"fact_ids\": [\"F-RIVERA-TRANSFER\"],\n      \"reason\": \"Muldrow rejected the significance test for discrimination but did not eliminate all harm; Burlington's material-adversity standard for retaliation remains controlling and was not lowered.\"\n    },\n    \"T-WORKED-DAYS\": {\n      \"verdict\": \"rejected\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"SRC-SCOTUS-WALTERS-1997\"],\n      \"fact_ids\": [\"F-PARTTIME-FRIDAY\", \"F-PAYROLL-16\"],\n      \"reason\": \"Walters holds the payroll method counts employment relationships, not whether the person worked or was paid that day; the part-timers' relationships continued on Fridays.\"\n    },\n    \"T-180-DAYS\": {\n      \"verdict\": \"rejected\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"SRC-STATUTE-2000E-5\", \"SRC-EEOC-FILING-CHARGE\"],\n      \"fact_ids\": [\"F-FILING-295\", \"F-FEPA\"],\n      \"reason\": \"Where an authorized state agency exists, the filing deadline extends to 300 days; Rivera's 295-day filing is timely.\"\n    },\n    \"T-SAFETY-PROTECTED\": {\n      \"verdict\": \"rejected\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"SRC-STATUTE-2000E-3\"],\n      \"fact_ids\": [\"F-LEE-SAFETY-ONLY\"],\n      \"reason\": \"Title VII protects opposition to enumerated discrimination (sex, race, etc.), not general workplace safety complaints; Lee's safety-only complaint is not protected activity under Title VII.\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"sources_used\": [\n    \"SRC-STATUTE-2000E\",\n    \"SRC-STATUTE-2000E-2\",\n    \"SRC-STATUTE-2000E-3\",\n    \"SRC-STATUTE-2000E-5\",\n    \"SRC-SCOTUS-WALTERS-1997\",\n    \"SRC-SCOTUS-MULDROW-2024\",\n    \"SRC-SCOTUS-BURLINGTON-2006\",\n    \"SRC-EEOC-FILING-CHARGE\"\n  ],\n  \"advice\": {\n    \"liability_position\": \"preliminary_plausibility_not_ultimate_liability\",\n    \"proof_limitations\": [\n      \"evidence_admissibility_unassessed\",\n      \"credibility_unassessed\",\n      \"discovery_incomplete\"\n    ],\n    \"scope_limitations\": [\n      \"state_and_other_federal_law_out_of_scope\",\n      \"remedies_not_quantified\"\n    ],\n    \"recommended_next_step\": \"prompt_qualified_counsel_or_eeoc_review\"\n  }\n}\n</FINAL_JSON>\n\n---\n\n**MEMORANDUM — PRELIMINARY TITLE VII ANALYSIS**  \n**Date of Analysis:** 2025-03-01  \n**Scope:** US Federal Title VII Only\n\n**I. COVERAGE — Northwind is a covered employer.**  \nUnder 42 U.S.C. § 2000e(b) [SRC-STATUTE-2000E], an employer must have 15+ employees for each working day in 20+ calendar weeks in the current or preceding year. Under *Walters v. Metropolitan Educational Enterprises*, 519 U.S. 202, 207-212 [SRC-SCOTUS-WALTERS-1997], the \"payroll method\" counts employment relationships, not whether the person worked or was paid that day. Facts F-PAYROLL-16, F-PARTTIME-FRIDAY, and F-WEEKS-22 establish 16 employees (including 2 part-timers whose relationships continued on Fridays) across 22 weeks in both years. The two part-timers count on Fridays under *Walters*. Northwind meets the 15/20 threshold. The adversarial note T-WORKED-DAYS is rejected: *Walters* holds the touchstone is the relationship, not daily work or pay.\n\n**II. DISCRIMINATION (RIVERA) — Plausible claim; not ultimate liability.**  \nUnder § 2000e-2(a)(1) [SRC-STATUTE-2000E-2], discrimination in terms/conditions of employment because of sex is prohibited. Post-*Muldrow v. City of St. Louis*, No. 22-193 (2024) [SRC-SCOTUS-MULDROW-2024], a transfer plaintiff need only show \"some injury respecting employment terms or conditions\" — no significant-harm test. The Eighth Circuit's materially-significant-disadvantage standard (*Muldrow*, 30 F.4th 680) was vacated. Facts F-RIVERA-TRANSFER (loss of schedule, supervisory work, take-home vehicle), F-MALE-REPLACEMENT (less experienced male replaced her), and F-MOTIVE-SEX (preserved message preferring a man for client-facing lead) establish plausible sex discrimination. Equal pay does not defeat the claim; *Muldrow* rejected the significance test. Adversarial notes T-STALE-CA8 and T-STANDARDS-CONFLATED are rejected: the 2022 Eighth Circuit standard was vacated, and *Muldrow* did not eliminate all harm — it rejected the significance threshold while retaining a \"some injury\" requirement.\n\n**III. RETALIATION (RIVERA) — Plausible claim; material adversity required.**  \nUnder § 2000e-3(a) [SRC-STATUTE-2000E-3], retaliation for opposing a practice unlawful under Title VII is prohibited. Rivera's 2024-04-22 complaint to HR about sex-based assignment of client leads [F-RIVERA-PROTECTED-COMPLAINT] is protected opposition. The transfer [F-RIVERA-TRANSFER] followed within 9 days, and an HR note attributes it to reducing friction after the complaint [F-MOTIVE-COMPLAINT]. Under *Burlington Northern*, 548 U.S. 53, 61-73 [SRC-SCOTUS-BURLINGTON-2006], retaliation requires material adversity that might dissuade a reasonable worker from making a charge; context matters. Loss of schedule, supervisory work, and vehicle plausibly meets that standard. *Muldrow*'s \"some injury\" test is specific to discrimination claims, not retaliation. Adversarial note T-STANDARDS-CONFLATED is rejected: *Muldrow* did not lower the retaliation standard.\n\n**IV. TIMELINESS (RIVERA) — Timely under 300-day FEPA window.**  \nUnder § 2000e-5(e)(1) [SRC-STATUTE-2000E-5] and EEOC guidance [SRC-EEOC-FILING-CHARGE], the ordinary deadline is 180 days, but in a jurisdiction with an authorized state agency, it extends to 300 days. Facts F-FILING-295 (295 days after final notice), F-FEPA (authorized state agency), and F-NO-FEPA-TERMINATION (no termination notice) place the filing within the 300-day window. The 2024-05-01 notice [F-NOTICE-DATE] is the actionable event; 295 days is within 300. Adversarial note T-180-DAYS is rejected: the 300-day extension applies where an authorized state agency exists.\n\n**V. RETALIATION (LEE) — No Title VII protected activity on supplied facts.**  \nUnder § 2000e-3(a) [SRC-STATUTE-2000E-3], protected activity requires opposition to a practice made unlawful by Title VII or participation in a Title VII proceeding. Fact F-LEE-SAFETY-ONLY states Lee complained only about physical safety and never opposed a Title VII-unlawful practice. Physical safety complaints, without more, are not Title VII-protected activity. Adversarial note T-SAFETY-PROTECTED is rejected: Title VII protects opposition to enumerated discrimination (sex, race, etc.), not general safety complaints. Lee's identical transfer does not support Title VII retaliation on these facts.",
